1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,880

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:10,800
So we spent sometimes like
two months training the bots

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:15,690
on thousands of CPUs,
terabytes of memory sometimes.

4
00:00:15,690 --> 00:00:17,310
But when it came
time to actually play

5
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:20,130
against the humans, they
would act almost instantly.

6
00:00:20,130 --> 00:00:22,780
It was just a lookup table.

7
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:26,050
And the humans, when they
were in a tough spot,

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:28,450
they would not act
instantly, they would think.

9
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:31,035
They would sit there and they
would think for five seconds,

10
00:00:31,035 --> 00:00:33,410
maybe five minutes if it was
a really difficult decision.

11
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:35,680
And it was clear that was
allowing them to come up

12
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,310
with better strategies.

13
00:00:38,310 --> 00:00:41,450
And so I wanted to investigate
this behavior in our bots.

14
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:45,337
If we could add this to our
bots, how much of a difference

15
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:45,920
would it make?

16
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,320
The ability to, instead
of acting instantly

17
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,280
to take some time and
compute a better strategy

18
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:56,220
for the spot that
the agent was in.

19
00:00:56,220 --> 00:00:58,620
And this is what I found.

20
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:01,933
So on the x-axis here, we
have the number of buckets.

21
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:03,600
You can think of this
as like the number

22
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:04,767
of parameters in your model.

23
00:01:04,767 --> 00:01:07,210
And on the y-axis, we have
distance from Nash equilibrium.

24
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:08,880
So this is basically
like how much you would

25
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,172
lose to a worst case adversary.

26
00:01:10,172 --> 00:01:13,393
So the lower this number is,
the better your poker bot is.

27
00:01:13,393 --> 00:01:15,810
And you can see, as you scale
up the number of parameters,

28
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:16,920
your performance improves.

29
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:21,987
And as you increase the number
parameters by about 100x,

30
00:01:21,987 --> 00:01:23,820
your exploitability
goes down by about half.

31
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:26,502
And indeed, you're getting
a much better poker bot.

32
00:01:26,502 --> 00:01:27,960
But you can see,
the blue line here

33
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:30,120
is if you don't have
search, and the orange line

34
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:32,080
is if you do add search.

35
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,660
And you can see,
just adding search,

36
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,030
adding the ability to sit
there and think for a bit

37
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:39,300
improved the performance
of these models

38
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:40,740
and it reduced the
exploitability,

39
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:43,980
the distance from Nash
equilibrium by about 7x.

40
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:47,220
And if you were to extend that
blue line and see how many

41
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:50,910
parameters would you need
in order to be comparable

42
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:54,210
to adding search, the answer
is you would need to scale up

43
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:58,440
your model by about 100,000x.

44
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:02,310
So this was pretty mind-blowing
to me when I saw this.

45
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:06,570
Over the course of my PhD, the
first three years, first three

46
00:02:06,570 --> 00:02:09,074
or four years in my PhD,
I managed to scale up

47
00:02:09,074 --> 00:02:10,199
these models by about 100x.

48
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:13,160

49
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,160
And I was proud of that.

50
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,610
I mean, that's a pretty
impressive result, I think.

51
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:22,230
But what this plot was showing
me was that just adding search

52
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:25,110
was the equivalent of scaling
things up by about 100,000x.

53
00:02:25,110 --> 00:02:27,930
And so all of my previous
research up until this point

54
00:02:27,930 --> 00:02:32,400
would just be a footnote
compared to adding search.

55
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,560
So when I saw this,
it became clear.

56
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,600
This was the answer to
beating top humans poker.

57
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:38,490
And so for the next
year, basically nonstop,

58
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:41,370
I worked on scaling search.

59
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:43,420
Now, there's a question
that naturally comes up,

60
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:45,360
which is why wasn't
this considered before?

61
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,300
There's a few factors.

62
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,105
So first of all, I should
say search had been

63
00:02:49,105 --> 00:02:50,230
considered in poker before.

64
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:52,740
And it's actually quite
natural to say, well,

65
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,930
if you had search
in chess and search

66
00:02:54,930 --> 00:02:59,153
in Go, why would you not
consider search in poker?

67
00:02:59,153 --> 00:03:00,070
There's a few reasons.

68
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:03,115
One is that, culturally,
the poker research

69
00:03:03,115 --> 00:03:05,240
grew out of game theory
and reinforcement learning.

70
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:07,240
And so it wasn't really
from the same background

71
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:11,370
as the people that are working
on chess and working on Go.

72
00:03:11,370 --> 00:03:14,040
When you scale search,
scaling test on compute,

73
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,590
it makes all your experiments
much more expensive and just

74
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:19,592
more unpleasant to work with.

75
00:03:19,592 --> 00:03:21,300
And there were just
incentive structures.

76
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:22,410
I mean, people always
thinking about winning

77
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:24,270
the next annual computer
poker competition.

78
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:25,980
And the ACPC limited
the resources

79
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:27,313
that you could use at test time.

80
00:03:27,313 --> 00:03:31,557
So search wasn't really possible
effectively in the ACPC.

81
00:03:31,557 --> 00:03:33,640
And I think the biggest
factor is that people just

82
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,330
didn't think it would make
such a huge difference.

83
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:37,270
I mean, I think it's
reasonable to look at something

84
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:39,270
like search and think
like, oh, yeah, that might

85
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:40,660
make a 10x difference.

86
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,247
You probably wouldn't think
it makes 100,000x difference.

87
00:03:43,247 --> 00:03:45,080
And so there were some
people working on it,

88
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:47,622
but it wasn't really the focus
of a lot of people's research.

89
00:03:47,622 --> 00:03:51,030

90
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:53,910
So anyway, focused on
scaling search, and that

91
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:57,990
led to the 2017 Brains versus
AI competition where we again

92
00:03:57,990 --> 00:04:01,020
played our bot against
four top poker pros.

93
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:04,560
120,000 hands of poker,
$200,000 in prize money.

94
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:07,650
And this time, the bot won
by 15 big blinds per 100

95
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:10,260
instead of nine
big blinds per 100.

96
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,990
This was a crushing victory.

97
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:14,760
Each team lost
individually to the bot

98
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:21,050
and four standard deviations
of statistical significance.

99
00:04:21,050 --> 00:04:24,290
We followed this up in 2019
with a Six-Player Poker AI

100
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:25,643
competition.

101
00:04:25,643 --> 00:04:27,560
The big difference here
is that we figured out

102
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:28,852
how to do depth-limited search.

103
00:04:28,852 --> 00:04:32,270
So before the 2017 bot, it
would always have the search

104
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:33,530
to the end of the game.

105
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:36,110
Here, it only had to do
search a few moves ahead

106
00:04:36,110 --> 00:04:37,560
and you can stop there.

107
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:40,588
And so this time, again, it won
with statistical significance.

108
00:04:40,588 --> 00:04:42,380
And what's really
surprising about this bot

109
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:45,590
is that despite it being
a much larger game,

110
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:48,650
the six-player poker bot
Pluribus cost under $150

111
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:52,430
to train on cloud
computing resources.

112
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:54,740
And it runs on 28 CPU
cores at different time.

113
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:56,860
There's no GPUs.

114
00:04:56,860 --> 00:05:01,498
So I think what this
shows is that this really

115
00:05:01,498 --> 00:05:02,790
wasn't algorithmic improvement.

116
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:05,060
I mean, this would have
been doable 20 years ago

117
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,560
if people knew how to do it.

118
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:12,850
And I think it also shows
the power of search.

119
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:14,830
If you can figure
out how to scale,

120
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:16,390
that compute at
test time, it really

121
00:05:16,390 --> 00:05:20,020
can make a huge difference and
bring down your training costs

122
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:23,170
by huge amount.

123
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:26,320
Anyway, so I want to say also,
this is not limited to poker.

124
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,700
If you look at Go, you
see a similar pattern.

125
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,353
So this is a plot from
the AlphaGo Zero paper.

126
00:05:31,353 --> 00:05:33,520
On the x-axis, we have
different versions of AlphaGo

127
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:35,080
and on the y-axis,
we have Elo rating,

128
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:36,830
which is a way of
comparing different bots

129
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,320
but also a way of
comparing bots to humans.

130
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:41,230
And you can see, if--

131
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:43,900

132
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:46,803
so super human performance
is around 3,600 Elo.

133
00:05:46,803 --> 00:05:48,970
And you can see AlphaGo
lead the version that played

134
00:05:48,970 --> 00:05:50,860
against Lisa Dole in 2016.

135
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:53,462
That's right over the line
of superhuman performance.

136
00:05:53,462 --> 00:05:55,420
AlphaGo zero, the strongest
version of AlphaGo,

137
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:58,360
is around 5200 Elo.

138
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:01,180
But if you take out
the test time search,

139
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:03,820
if you just play according
to the policy net

140
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:07,000
and not do any Monte Carlo tree
search in AlphaGo Zero at test

141
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,910
time, then the Elo rating
drops to around 3,000,

142
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:15,070
which is substantially below
expert human performance.

143
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:17,640
So what this shows is that if
you take out Monte Carlo tree

144
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:22,590
search at test time, the
AlphaGo Zero is not superhuman.

145
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:25,680
And in fact, nobody
has made a superhuman

146
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:29,070
Go bot that does not
use search in some form.

147
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:31,650
Nobody has made a
raw neural network

148
00:06:31,650 --> 00:06:34,557
that can beat top humans in Go.

149
00:06:34,557 --> 00:06:36,890
And I should say also, this
is just if you're taking out

150
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:37,850
the search at test time.

151
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:40,250
I'm not even talking about
taking it out of training time.

152
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:41,150
If you took it out
of training time,

153
00:06:41,150 --> 00:06:42,650
it wouldn't even
get off the ground.

154
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:44,930

155
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:47,075
Now, there's a question
of, OK, well, surely you

156
00:06:47,075 --> 00:06:48,700
could just scale up
the model, scale up

157
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,070
the amount of training,
and you would eventually

158
00:06:51,070 --> 00:06:54,340
surpass superhuman performance
and match the performance

159
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:55,450
if you added search.

160
00:06:55,450 --> 00:06:56,470
And that's true, yes.

161
00:06:56,470 --> 00:06:59,770
If you scale up the models and
if you scale up the training,

162
00:06:59,770 --> 00:07:02,583
then you would eventually match
the performance with search.

163
00:07:02,583 --> 00:07:04,000
But there's a
question of how much

164
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,768
would you have to
scale it up by?

165
00:07:05,768 --> 00:07:07,560
Now, a rough rule of
thumb is that in order

166
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:10,200
to increase your Elo
rating by about 120 points,

167
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,660
you either have to double
the amount of model size

168
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:15,077
and training or you have to
double the amount of test time

169
00:07:15,077 --> 00:07:16,650
search.

170
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:19,460
So if you look at that gap of
around 2000 Elo points and you

171
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:21,710
calculate the number of
doublings that you would need,

172
00:07:21,710 --> 00:07:24,320
the answer is that in order
to get the raw policy net from

173
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:27,860
3,000 to 5,200 Elo, you would
need to scale your model

174
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:34,940
and your training
by about 100,000x.

175
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:37,290
So why is this important?

176
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:39,110
I think you look at
what's happening today

177
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:41,660
with large language
models and transformers

178
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:42,980
and you see something similar.

179
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:45,680
I mean you're getting huge--

180
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:49,100
there's a question of,
what do I mean by search?

181
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:50,500
There's specific
kinds of search,

182
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,000
like Monte Carlo tree search,
the ability to just plan ahead

183
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,600
what you're going to do instead
of just acting instantly based

184
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:57,890
on your pre-computed policy.

185
00:07:57,890 --> 00:07:59,930
But really, what I mean
by search more broadly

186
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:03,590
is the ability to scale
the amount of computation

187
00:08:03,590 --> 00:08:06,140
to get better performance.

188
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:09,020
I think that's the real
value that search is adding.

189
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:13,400
Instead of just acting
according to your pre-computed--

190
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:15,055
front-loading all
of your computation,

191
00:08:15,055 --> 00:08:16,430
so that you're
doing everything--

192
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:17,847
all your computation
ahead of time

193
00:08:17,847 --> 00:08:22,490
and then add inference time
acting basically instantly.

194
00:08:22,490 --> 00:08:24,350
Could you get a
better solution if you

195
00:08:24,350 --> 00:08:27,170
had five minutes to
output an action instead

196
00:08:27,170 --> 00:08:31,540
of 100 milliseconds?

197
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:35,809
So, yeah, I think you look at--

198
00:08:35,809 --> 00:08:37,767
sorry, there's a question.

199
00:08:37,767 --> 00:08:40,100
Does a transformer with a
search circuit count as search

200
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:43,560
or do you mean hand
engineering search algos?

201
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:45,560
I don't want to get bogged
down into the details

202
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:49,580
of how to do this because the
answer is nobody really knows

203
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:50,120
yet.

204
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,120
Nobody really has a general
way of doing search.

205
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:54,530
In all the domains that we've
done search successfully,

206
00:08:54,530 --> 00:08:59,040
like poker and Go, it's done in
a fairly domain-specific way.

207
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:03,430
Go use this algorithm called
Monte Carlo tree search.

208
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:05,430
And yeah, I guess you
could think of beam search

209
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:06,847
as like one simple
form of search.

210
00:09:06,847 --> 00:09:08,640
But it does seem
like there should

211
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:14,460
be better ways in the future.

212
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:16,040
So anyway, where
I'm going with this

213
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:19,400
is look at how large language
models are being trained today.

214
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:22,490
And you're seeing
millions of dollars

215
00:09:22,490 --> 00:09:25,660
being thrown at pre-training.

216
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:29,440
I wouldn't be surprised
if we see a large language

217
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:32,500
model that would cost
$100 million to train.

218
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:34,890
We might even get
to $1,000,000,000.

219
00:09:34,890 --> 00:09:39,765
But the inference cost is
still going to be very small.

220
00:09:39,765 --> 00:09:41,390
And so there's a
question of, could you

221
00:09:41,390 --> 00:09:46,340
do substantially better if
you could scale the amount

222
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:49,070
of inference cost as well?

223
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:54,230
You may amortize some
of your training cost.

224
00:09:54,230 --> 00:09:57,910
So there's this lecture
called the Bitter Lesson

225
00:09:57,910 --> 00:10:00,160
by Richard Sutton that says,
"The biggest lesson that

226
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:02,140
can be learned--" and so
it's a really great essay.

227
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:03,140
I recommend reading it.

228
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:04,510
But one of the big
takeaways is he

229
00:10:04,510 --> 00:10:06,160
says, "The biggest lesson that
can be learned from over 70

230
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,993
years of AI research
is that general methods

231
00:10:07,993 --> 00:10:10,613
that leverage computation are
ultimately the most effective.

232
00:10:10,613 --> 00:10:13,030
The two methods that seem to
scale arbitrarily in this way

233
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:15,810
are search and learning."

234
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:20,142
Now, I think we've done a great
job with generalizing search--

235
00:10:20,142 --> 00:10:21,350
sorry, generalizing learning.

236
00:10:21,350 --> 00:10:22,820
And I think that there is
still room for improvement

237
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:23,820
when it comes to search.

238
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:27,540

239
00:10:27,540 --> 00:10:29,880
And yeah, the next goal
really is about generality.

240
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:32,173
Can we develop a truly general
way of scaling inference

241
00:10:32,173 --> 00:10:34,590
compute instead of just doing
things like Monte Carlo tree

242
00:10:34,590 --> 00:10:36,570
search that are
specific to a better

243
00:10:36,570 --> 00:10:39,720
domain-- to a specific domain,
and also better than things

244
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:40,710
like chain of thought?

245
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:43,652

246
00:10:43,652 --> 00:10:46,110
What this would look like is
that you have much higher test

247
00:10:46,110 --> 00:10:48,070
time compute.

248
00:10:48,070 --> 00:10:50,690
But you have much
more capable models.

249
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:53,320
And I think for certain domains,
that trade off is worth it.

250
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:54,695
If you think about
what inference

251
00:10:54,695 --> 00:10:57,040
cost we're willing to pay
for a proof of the Riemann

252
00:10:57,040 --> 00:11:00,310
hypothesis, I think we'd
be willing to pay a lot.

253
00:11:00,310 --> 00:11:02,815
Or the cost of--

254
00:11:02,815 --> 00:11:05,190
what cost are we willing to
pay for new lifesaving drugs?

255
00:11:05,190 --> 00:11:07,600
I think we'd be
willing to pay a lot.

256
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:11,520
So I think that there
is an opportunity here.

257
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:13,205
Anyway, so that's in prelude--

258
00:11:13,205 --> 00:11:14,580
I guess, any
questions about that

259
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:15,705
before I move on to Cicero?

260
00:11:15,705 --> 00:11:24,268

261
00:11:24,268 --> 00:11:26,310
By the way, the reason
why I'm talking about this

262
00:11:26,310 --> 00:11:30,670
is because it's going to inform
the approach that we took

263
00:11:30,670 --> 00:11:33,550
to Cicero, which
I think is quite

264
00:11:33,550 --> 00:11:36,940
different from the approach
that a lot of other researchers

265
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:40,030
might have taken
to this problem.

266
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:42,030
Someone asks, can you
give an example of search?

267
00:11:42,030 --> 00:11:44,400
Well, Monte Carlo tree
search is one form of search.

268
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:48,303
You can also think of breadth
for search, depth for search,

269
00:11:48,303 --> 00:11:49,220
these kinds of things.

270
00:11:49,220 --> 00:11:51,000
They're all search.

271
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,950
I would also argue
that chain of thought

272
00:11:52,950 --> 00:11:55,050
is doing something
similar to search

273
00:11:55,050 --> 00:11:58,410
where it's allowing the model to
leverage extra compute at test

274
00:11:58,410 --> 00:12:01,837
time to get better performance.

275
00:12:01,837 --> 00:12:03,420
But I think that
that's the main thing

276
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:05,920
that you want, the ability to
leverage extra compute at test

277
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:08,000
time.

278
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,560
What's the search--
what's the space

279
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:11,240
that you are searching over?

280
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:14,570
Again, in a game like Go, it's
different board positions.

281
00:12:14,570 --> 00:12:17,000
But you could also
imagine searching

282
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,230
over different sentences that
you could say things like that.

283
00:12:21,230 --> 00:12:24,380

284
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:26,210
There's a lot of
flexibility there as well.

285
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:31,030

286
00:12:31,030 --> 00:12:32,923
So now, I want to
get into Cicero.

287
00:12:32,923 --> 00:12:35,090
So first thing I should say
when it comes to Cicero,

288
00:12:35,090 --> 00:12:38,980
this is a big team effort.

289
00:12:38,980 --> 00:12:43,053

290
00:12:43,053 --> 00:12:44,720
This is actually one
of the great things

291
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:46,100
about working on this
project, that there was just

292
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:48,860
such a diverse talent pool
experts in reinforcement

293
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:52,170
learning, planning, game theory,
natural language processing,

294
00:12:52,170 --> 00:12:53,970
all working together on this.

295
00:12:53,970 --> 00:12:58,370
And it would not have been
possible without everybody.

296
00:12:58,370 --> 00:13:01,220
So the motivation for Diplomacy
actually came from 2019.

297
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:03,020
We were looking at
all the breakthroughs

298
00:13:03,020 --> 00:13:04,353
that were happening at the time.

299
00:13:04,353 --> 00:13:08,030
And I think a good example of
this XKCD comic that came out

300
00:13:08,030 --> 00:13:11,380
in 2012 that shows different
categories games, games that

301
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:13,130
are solved, games where
computers can beat

302
00:13:13,130 --> 00:13:15,380
top humans, games for computers
to lose to top humans,

303
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:17,935
and games where computers
may never outplay top humans.

304
00:13:17,935 --> 00:13:19,310
And in this category
of computers

305
00:13:19,310 --> 00:13:22,790
to lose to top humans, you had
four games, Go, Arimaa, poker,

306
00:13:22,790 --> 00:13:25,700
and StarCraft.

307
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:30,240
In 2015, actually, one of
my colleagues, David Wu,

308
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:32,790
made the first AI to beat
top humans in Arimaa.

309
00:13:32,790 --> 00:13:36,570
In 2016, we have AlphaGo
beating Lisa Dole on Go.

310
00:13:36,570 --> 00:13:38,910
In 2017, you have the work
that I just described where

311
00:13:38,910 --> 00:13:41,010
we beat top humans in poker.

312
00:13:41,010 --> 00:13:46,050
And in 2019, we had an
AlphaStar beating expert humans

313
00:13:46,050 --> 00:13:48,420
in StarCraft.

314
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:50,850
So that shows the incredible
amount of progress

315
00:13:50,850 --> 00:13:53,430
that had happened in
strategic reasoning

316
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:56,880
over the past several
years leading up to 2019.

317
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:01,350
And at the same time, we also
had GPT-2 come out in 2019.

318
00:14:01,350 --> 00:14:04,507
And it showed that language
model and natural language

319
00:14:04,507 --> 00:14:06,090
processing was
progressing much faster

320
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:08,298
than I think a lot of people,
including us, expected.

321
00:14:08,298 --> 00:14:10,610

322
00:14:10,610 --> 00:14:12,430
And so we were
thinking about what--

323
00:14:12,430 --> 00:14:14,085
after the six-player
poker work, I

324
00:14:14,085 --> 00:14:15,460
was discussing
with my colleagues

325
00:14:15,460 --> 00:14:17,210
what should we work on next?

326
00:14:17,210 --> 00:14:22,420
And we were throwing around
different domains to work on.

327
00:14:22,420 --> 00:14:25,780
And given the incredible
amount of progress in AI,

328
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:28,000
we wanted to pick
something really ambitious,

329
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,032
something that we
thought you couldn't just

330
00:14:31,032 --> 00:14:32,740
tackle by scaling up
existing approaches,

331
00:14:32,740 --> 00:14:35,890
that you really needed something
new in order to address.

332
00:14:35,890 --> 00:14:37,720
And we landed on
Diplomacy because we

333
00:14:37,720 --> 00:14:41,880
thought that it would be the
hardest game to make an AI for.

334
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:44,410
So what is Diplomacy?

335
00:14:44,410 --> 00:14:47,790
Diplomacy is a natural
language strategy game.

336
00:14:47,790 --> 00:14:50,610
It takes place right
before World War I.

337
00:14:50,610 --> 00:14:53,100
You play as one of the seven
great powers of Europe,

338
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:55,890
England, France, Germany,
Austria, Russia, and Turkey.

339
00:14:55,890 --> 00:14:59,700
And your goal is to control
the majority of the map.

340
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:02,040
In practice that
rarely happens, if you

341
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:03,930
control a majority of
the map then you've

342
00:15:03,930 --> 00:15:08,610
won, in practice, nobody
ends up winning outright.

343
00:15:08,610 --> 00:15:11,850
And so your score is
proportional to the percentage

344
00:15:11,850 --> 00:15:14,540
of the map that you control.

345
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:16,580
Now, what's really
interesting about Diplomacy

346
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:20,518
is that it is a natural
language negotiation game.

347
00:15:20,518 --> 00:15:23,060
So you have these conversations,
like what you're seeing here

348
00:15:23,060 --> 00:15:25,220
between Germany and England
where they will privately

349
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:27,470
communicate with each other
before making their moves.

350
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:30,590
And so you can have Germany
ask, want to support Sweden?

351
00:15:30,590 --> 00:15:35,830
England says, let me
think on that, and so on.

352
00:15:35,830 --> 00:15:40,590
So this is a popular strategy
game developed in the 1950s.

353
00:15:40,590 --> 00:15:44,090
It was JFK and Kissinger's
favorite game actually.

354
00:15:44,090 --> 00:15:46,040
And like I said,
each turn involves

355
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:49,020
sophisticated private natural
language negotiations.

356
00:15:49,020 --> 00:15:53,013
And I want to make clear,
this is not negotiations

357
00:15:53,013 --> 00:15:55,180
like you would see in a
game like Settlers of Catan,

358
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:56,110
for example.

359
00:15:56,110 --> 00:16:01,030
You're seeing-- it's
much more like Survivor,

360
00:16:01,030 --> 00:16:03,760
if you've ever seen
the TV show Survivor.

361
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:06,160
You have discussions
around alliances

362
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:08,470
that you'd like to
build, discussions

363
00:16:08,470 --> 00:16:10,120
around specific
tactics that you'd

364
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:14,500
like to execute on the current
turn, and also more long-term

365
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:17,140
strategy around where
do we go from here

366
00:16:17,140 --> 00:16:19,570
and how do we divide resources.

367
00:16:19,570 --> 00:16:23,043
Now, the way the game works,
you have these negotiations

368
00:16:23,043 --> 00:16:24,960
that lasts between 5 and
15 minutes, depending

369
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:28,620
on the version of the
game on each turn.

370
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:30,570
And all these negotiations
are done privately,

371
00:16:30,570 --> 00:16:31,740
pairwise negotiation.

372
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:34,600
Also, I think that
you are not muted.

373
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:36,780
Thank you.

374
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:38,910
And then after the
negotiation period completes,

375
00:16:38,910 --> 00:16:41,788
everybody will simultaneously
write down their moves.

376
00:16:41,788 --> 00:16:43,830
And so a player could
promise you something like,

377
00:16:43,830 --> 00:16:46,842
I'm going to support you into
this territory in this turn.

378
00:16:46,842 --> 00:16:49,050
But then when people actually
write down their moves,

379
00:16:49,050 --> 00:16:50,350
they might not write that down.

380
00:16:50,350 --> 00:16:52,770
And so you only find out if
they were true to their word

381
00:16:52,770 --> 00:16:55,548
when all the moves are
revealed simultaneously.

382
00:16:55,548 --> 00:16:58,540

383
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:01,590
And so for this reason,
alliances and trust-building

384
00:17:01,590 --> 00:17:03,954
is key, the ability
to trust that somebody

385
00:17:03,954 --> 00:17:05,829
is going to follow
through on their promises.

386
00:17:05,829 --> 00:17:07,579
That's really what
this game is all about.

387
00:17:07,579 --> 00:17:09,131
And the ability
to convince people

388
00:17:09,131 --> 00:17:11,339
that you are going to follow
through on your promises

389
00:17:11,339 --> 00:17:14,910
is really what this
game is all about.

390
00:17:14,910 --> 00:17:16,783
And so for this
reason, Diplomacy

391
00:17:16,783 --> 00:17:18,950
has long been considered a
challenge problem for AI.

392
00:17:18,950 --> 00:17:20,992
This research and the game
going back to the 80s,

393
00:17:20,992 --> 00:17:23,880
the research really
only picked up--

394
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:27,089
it picked up quite
intensely starting in 2019

395
00:17:27,089 --> 00:17:31,830
when researchers from DeepMind,
ourselves, Mila, other places

396
00:17:31,830 --> 00:17:33,540
started working on this.

397
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:35,153
Now, a lot of that research--

398
00:17:35,153 --> 00:17:36,570
the vast majority
of that research

399
00:17:36,570 --> 00:17:38,820
actually was focused on
the non-language version

400
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:40,350
of the game, which was
seen as a stepping stone

401
00:17:40,350 --> 00:17:41,892
to the full natural
language version.

402
00:17:41,892 --> 00:17:44,520
Though we decided to
focus from the start

403
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:49,090
on the full natural language
version of the game.

404
00:17:49,090 --> 00:17:52,180
So to give you a sense of what
these negotiations and dialogue

405
00:17:52,180 --> 00:17:55,280
look like, here is one example.

406
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:59,920
So here, England, you can
see they move their fleet

407
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:02,570
in Norway to Saint Petersburg.

408
00:18:02,570 --> 00:18:05,590
And that occupies the
Russian territory.

409
00:18:05,590 --> 00:18:07,090
And so this is what
the border state

410
00:18:07,090 --> 00:18:08,410
looks like after that move.

411
00:18:08,410 --> 00:18:10,225
And now, there's
this conversation

412
00:18:10,225 --> 00:18:11,350
between Austria and Russia.

413
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:13,750
Austria says, well,
what happened up North?

414
00:18:13,750 --> 00:18:15,550
Russia says, England stabbed.

415
00:18:15,550 --> 00:18:17,110
I'm afraid that they may
be closer to me, my friend.

416
00:18:17,110 --> 00:18:18,100
Austria says,
yeah, that's rough.

417
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:19,430
Are you going to be OK up there?

418
00:18:19,430 --> 00:18:20,388
Russia says, I hope so.

419
00:18:20,388 --> 00:18:22,272
England seems to still
want to work together.

420
00:18:22,272 --> 00:18:24,230
Austria says, can you
make a deal with Germany?

421
00:18:24,230 --> 00:18:25,990
So the players
are now discussing

422
00:18:25,990 --> 00:18:28,930
what should be discussed
with other players.

423
00:18:28,930 --> 00:18:30,102
Russia says, good idea.

424
00:18:30,102 --> 00:18:31,810
Then Austria says,
you'll be fine as long

425
00:18:31,810 --> 00:18:33,087
as you can defend Sevastopol.

426
00:18:33,087 --> 00:18:35,170
So Sevastopol is this
territory down to the South.

427
00:18:35,170 --> 00:18:39,250
You can see that Turkey has a
fleet and an army in the Black

428
00:18:39,250 --> 00:18:41,140
Sea in Armenia
next to Sevastopol.

429
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:43,630
And so they could potentially
attack that territory

430
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:46,755
next turn.

431
00:18:46,755 --> 00:18:48,630
Austria says, can you
support hold Sevastopol

432
00:18:48,630 --> 00:18:49,672
with Ukraine and Romania?

433
00:18:49,672 --> 00:18:51,290
I'll support hold Romania.

434
00:18:51,290 --> 00:18:52,950
Russia says, yep,
I'm already doing so.

435
00:18:52,950 --> 00:18:54,260
Austria says, awesome.

436
00:18:54,260 --> 00:18:57,055
Hopefully, we can start
getting you back on your feet.

437
00:18:57,055 --> 00:18:59,180
So this is an example of
the kinds of conversations

438
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:00,980
that you'll see in
a game of Diplomacy.

439
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:05,010
In this conversation, Austria
is actually our bot Cicero.

440
00:19:05,010 --> 00:19:07,730
So that kind of gives you a
sense of the sophistication

441
00:19:07,730 --> 00:19:09,290
of the agents dialogue.

442
00:19:09,290 --> 00:19:14,920

443
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:16,060
OK, I'll skip this for--

444
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:17,990
so I guess I'll go into this.

445
00:19:17,990 --> 00:19:20,183
I don't want to take
up too much time.

446
00:19:20,183 --> 00:19:21,850
Really, what makes
diplomacy interesting

447
00:19:21,850 --> 00:19:23,060
is that support is key.

448
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:27,285
So here, for example,
Budapest and Warsaw, the red

449
00:19:27,285 --> 00:19:29,410
and the purple units both
try to move into Galicia.

450
00:19:29,410 --> 00:19:30,940
And so since it's
a one versus one,

451
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:32,590
they both bounced
back and neither

452
00:19:32,590 --> 00:19:34,240
moves into the territory.

453
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:37,088
In the middle panel, you can
see Vienna supports Budapest

454
00:19:37,088 --> 00:19:37,630
into Galicia.

455
00:19:37,630 --> 00:19:39,670
And so now, it's
a two versus one.

456
00:19:39,670 --> 00:19:43,075
And that red unit will
indeed enter Galicia.

457
00:19:43,075 --> 00:19:44,950
And what's really
interesting about Diplomacy

458
00:19:44,950 --> 00:19:47,200
is that it doesn't just have
to be your own units that

459
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:50,120
are supporting you, it could be
another player's units as well.

460
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:53,110
So for example, the green player
could support the red player

461
00:19:53,110 --> 00:19:56,990
into Galicia, and then that red
unit would still go in there.

462
00:19:56,990 --> 00:19:58,990
So support is really what
the game is all about,

463
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:01,115
and negotiating over support.

464
00:20:01,115 --> 00:20:02,490
And so for that
reason, Diplomacy

465
00:20:02,490 --> 00:20:05,298
has this reputation as the
game that ruins friendships.

466
00:20:05,298 --> 00:20:07,590
It's really difficult to have
an alliance with somebody

467
00:20:07,590 --> 00:20:11,890
for three or four hours and
then have them backstab you,

468
00:20:11,890 --> 00:20:14,710
and basically just
ruin your game.

469
00:20:14,710 --> 00:20:16,720
But if you talk to
expert Diplomacy players,

470
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:18,380
they view it differently.

471
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:20,680
They say diplomacy is
ultimately about building trust

472
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:22,763
in an environment that
encourages you to not trust

473
00:20:22,763 --> 00:20:24,553
anyone.

474
00:20:24,553 --> 00:20:26,470
And that's why we decided
to work on the game.

475
00:20:26,470 --> 00:20:28,990
Could we make an AI that
is able to build trust

476
00:20:28,990 --> 00:20:31,300
with the players in an
environment that encourages

477
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:33,010
them to not trust anybody?

478
00:20:33,010 --> 00:20:36,400
Can the bot honestly
communicate that it's

479
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:39,228
going to do something
and evaluate

480
00:20:39,228 --> 00:20:41,020
whether another person
is being honest when

481
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,145
they are saying that they're
going to do something?

482
00:20:43,145 --> 00:20:45,970

483
00:20:45,970 --> 00:20:48,200
So why Diplomacy?

484
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:50,290
It's this nice intersection
of reinforcement

485
00:20:50,290 --> 00:20:53,200
learning and planning,
and also natural language.

486
00:20:53,200 --> 00:20:56,048
There's two perspectives that
we can take on why Diplomacy

487
00:20:56,048 --> 00:20:57,340
is a really interesting domain.

488
00:20:57,340 --> 00:20:59,270
One is the multi-agent
perspective.

489
00:20:59,270 --> 00:21:03,430
So here, all the previous
gaming AI results,

490
00:21:03,430 --> 00:21:05,920
like chess, Go,
poker, these have all

491
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,080
been in purely zero sum--

492
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:09,700
two-player zero-sum domains.

493
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:12,610
And in these
domains, self-play is

494
00:21:12,610 --> 00:21:14,655
guaranteed to converge
to an optimal solution.

495
00:21:14,655 --> 00:21:16,030
Basically, what
this means is you

496
00:21:16,030 --> 00:21:18,460
can start having the bot
play completely from scratch

497
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:19,930
with no human data.

498
00:21:19,930 --> 00:21:22,000
And by playing against
itself repeatedly,

499
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,790
it will eventually converge
to this unbeatable optimal

500
00:21:24,790 --> 00:21:27,250
solution called the
Minimax equilibrium.

501
00:21:27,250 --> 00:21:30,280
But that result only holds
in two-player zero-sum games.

502
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:34,220
That whole paradigm only holds
in two-player zero-sum games.

503
00:21:34,220 --> 00:21:36,500
When you go to domains
that involve cooperation,

504
00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:39,080
in addition to
competition, then success

505
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:41,660
requires understanding human
behavior and conventions.

506
00:21:41,660 --> 00:21:44,300
You can't just treat the other
players like machines anymore.

507
00:21:44,300 --> 00:21:46,940
You have to treat
them like humans.

508
00:21:46,940 --> 00:21:49,340
You have to model
human irrationality,

509
00:21:49,340 --> 00:21:51,560
human suboptimality.

510
00:21:51,560 --> 00:21:55,072
One example of this
is actually language.

511
00:21:55,072 --> 00:21:57,280
You can imagine if you were
to train a bot completely

512
00:21:57,280 --> 00:21:59,573
from scratch in the
game of Diplomacy,

513
00:21:59,573 --> 00:22:01,490
the full natural language
version of the game,

514
00:22:01,490 --> 00:22:03,430
there's no reason why
the bot would learn

515
00:22:03,430 --> 00:22:04,513
to communicate in English.

516
00:22:04,513 --> 00:22:06,972
It would learn to communicate
in some weird gibberish robot

517
00:22:06,972 --> 00:22:07,570
language.

518
00:22:07,570 --> 00:22:09,818
And then when you stick it
in a game with six humans,

519
00:22:09,818 --> 00:22:11,860
it's not going to be able
to cooperate with them.

520
00:22:11,860 --> 00:22:15,570

521
00:22:15,570 --> 00:22:17,900
So we have to find a way
to incorporate human data

522
00:22:17,900 --> 00:22:21,200
and be able to learn how
humans behave in order

523
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:22,340
to succeed in this game.

524
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:27,580

525
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:29,900
There's also the
NLP perspective,

526
00:22:29,900 --> 00:22:32,830
which is that current language
models are essentially

527
00:22:32,830 --> 00:22:34,660
just imitating human-like text.

528
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:38,020
Now, there's been some
progress with things like RLHF.

529
00:22:38,020 --> 00:22:42,528
But that's still not really the
way that humans communicate.

530
00:22:42,528 --> 00:22:44,320
They communicate with
an intention in mind.

531
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:45,460
They come up with this
intention and then

532
00:22:45,460 --> 00:22:47,350
they communicate with
the goal of communicating

533
00:22:47,350 --> 00:22:47,975
that intention.

534
00:22:47,975 --> 00:22:51,290
And they understand that others
are trying to do the same.

535
00:22:51,290 --> 00:22:52,790
And so there's a
question of, can we

536
00:22:52,790 --> 00:22:57,320
move beyond chitchat to
grounded intentional dialogue?

537
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:00,800

538
00:23:00,800 --> 00:23:03,620
So Cicero is an AI
agent for Diplomacy

539
00:23:03,620 --> 00:23:06,260
that integrates high level
strategic play and open domain

540
00:23:06,260 --> 00:23:07,310
dialogue.

541
00:23:07,310 --> 00:23:09,380
And we use 50,000 human
games of Diplomacy

542
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:11,338
acquired through a
partnership with the website

543
00:23:11,338 --> 00:23:14,330
webdiplomacy.net.

544
00:23:14,330 --> 00:23:16,940
So we entered Cicero in an
online diplomacy league,

545
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:19,160
just to give you the
results up front.

546
00:23:19,160 --> 00:23:21,020
Cicero was not
detected as an AI agent

547
00:23:21,020 --> 00:23:23,840
for 40 games with
82 unique players.

548
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:27,410
There was one player that
mentioned after the fact that--

549
00:23:27,410 --> 00:23:30,657
they kind of made a joke
about us being a bot.

550
00:23:30,657 --> 00:23:32,240
But they didn't
really follow up on it

551
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:33,450
and nobody else
followed up on it.

552
00:23:33,450 --> 00:23:35,825
And they later accused somebody
else of also being a bot.

553
00:23:35,825 --> 00:23:38,200
So we weren't sure how seriously
to take that accusation.

554
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:40,700
But I think it's safe to say
it made it through all 40 games

555
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:42,560
without being detected as bot.

556
00:23:42,560 --> 00:23:44,990
And then in fact, when we
told the players afterwards

557
00:23:44,990 --> 00:23:46,820
that it was a bot
the whole time,

558
00:23:46,820 --> 00:23:49,520
these are the kinds of
responses that we got.

559
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:52,640
People were quite surprised,
pleasantly surprised,

560
00:23:52,640 --> 00:23:53,450
fortunately.

561
00:23:53,450 --> 00:23:55,490
Nobody was upset with us.

562
00:23:55,490 --> 00:23:58,390
But they were quite
surprised that there

563
00:23:58,390 --> 00:24:00,220
was a bot that had
been playing this game

564
00:24:00,220 --> 00:24:02,960
within the whole time.

565
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:04,630
So in terms of
results, Cicero placed

566
00:24:04,630 --> 00:24:07,450
in the top 10% of players.

567
00:24:07,450 --> 00:24:08,660
It's a high variance game.

568
00:24:08,660 --> 00:24:11,800
And so if you look at players
that played five or more games,

569
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:13,420
it placed 2nd out of 19.

570
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:16,470
And it achieved more than
double the average human score.

571
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:18,630
So I would describe
this as a strong level

572
00:24:18,630 --> 00:24:19,650
of human performance.

573
00:24:19,650 --> 00:24:22,260
I wouldn't go as far as to
say that this is superhuman,

574
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:23,670
by any means.

575
00:24:23,670 --> 00:24:30,540
But it is currently
quite a strong result.

576
00:24:30,540 --> 00:24:35,380
Now, to give you a picture
of how Cicero works.

577
00:24:35,380 --> 00:24:39,150
So the input that we
feed into the model

578
00:24:39,150 --> 00:24:41,940
is the board state and
the recent action history.

579
00:24:41,940 --> 00:24:43,410
That's shown at
the top left here,

580
00:24:43,410 --> 00:24:46,830
and also the dialogue that it's
had with all the players up

581
00:24:46,830 --> 00:24:49,390
until now.

582
00:24:49,390 --> 00:24:52,260
So that's going to get fed into
a dialogue conditional action

583
00:24:52,260 --> 00:24:56,610
model that's going to
predict what Cicero thinks

584
00:24:56,610 --> 00:24:58,620
all the players are
going to do this turn

585
00:24:58,620 --> 00:25:01,110
and what they think
we will do this turn.

586
00:25:01,110 --> 00:25:05,870

587
00:25:05,870 --> 00:25:08,495
These leads what we call
anchor policies that

588
00:25:08,495 --> 00:25:09,620
are then used for planning.

589
00:25:09,620 --> 00:25:13,790

590
00:25:13,790 --> 00:25:17,120
Now, planning here,
again, this is

591
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:21,200
like the part where we leverage
extra compute at test time

592
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:22,908
in order to get
better performance.

593
00:25:22,908 --> 00:25:24,950
So essentially, we take
these initial predictions

594
00:25:24,950 --> 00:25:26,492
of what everybody's
going to do, what

595
00:25:26,492 --> 00:25:28,670
are called anchor policies.

596
00:25:28,670 --> 00:25:32,300
And we improve upon these
predictions using this planning

597
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:34,400
process called piKL,
where basically we

598
00:25:34,400 --> 00:25:38,025
account for the fact
that players will pick

599
00:25:38,025 --> 00:25:39,650
actions that have
higher expected value

600
00:25:39,650 --> 00:25:40,760
with higher probability.

601
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:43,250
We're essentially adding
this rationality prior

602
00:25:43,250 --> 00:25:45,530
to all the players to
assume that they're not

603
00:25:45,530 --> 00:25:47,852
going to blunder as often
as the model might suggest,

604
00:25:47,852 --> 00:25:50,060
and they're going to pick
smarter actions with higher

605
00:25:50,060 --> 00:25:52,242
probability than the
initial model might suggest.

606
00:25:52,242 --> 00:25:54,200
And what we find is that
this actually gives us

607
00:25:54,200 --> 00:25:56,420
a better prediction of
what all the players will

608
00:25:56,420 --> 00:25:59,870
do than just relying on
the raw neural net itself.

609
00:25:59,870 --> 00:26:03,450

610
00:26:03,450 --> 00:26:06,130
This gives us the action that
we actually play in the game.

611
00:26:06,130 --> 00:26:09,420
And it also gives us
what we call "intense."

612
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:11,370
So intense are an
action for ourselves

613
00:26:11,370 --> 00:26:14,910
and an action for the dialogue
partner that we're speaking to.

614
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:17,580
And now, we have this
dialogue conditional--

615
00:26:17,580 --> 00:26:20,620
so we have this dialogue
model that conditions on these

616
00:26:20,620 --> 00:26:21,120
"intense."

617
00:26:21,120 --> 00:26:24,627
So the intense are fed into
the dialogue model along

618
00:26:24,627 --> 00:26:27,210
with the board state and action
history, and also the dialogue

619
00:26:27,210 --> 00:26:28,985
that we've had so far.

620
00:26:28,985 --> 00:26:30,360
And that dialogue
model will then

621
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:34,760
generate candidate
messages that our

622
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:37,837
conditioned on those intense.

623
00:26:37,837 --> 00:26:39,670
These candidate messages
go through a series

624
00:26:39,670 --> 00:26:41,680
of filters that
filter out nonsense,

625
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:44,560
grounding issues, and also
low expected value action--

626
00:26:44,560 --> 00:26:46,270
low expected value messages.

627
00:26:46,270 --> 00:26:48,700
And ultimately, we
get out a message

628
00:26:48,700 --> 00:26:52,140
to send to our dialogue partner.

629
00:26:52,140 --> 00:26:54,060
Now, every time we send
or receive a message,

630
00:26:54,060 --> 00:26:55,477
we will repeat
this whole process.

631
00:26:55,477 --> 00:26:59,870

632
00:26:59,870 --> 00:27:04,120
So there's actually a lot
that is quite novel in Cicero.

633
00:27:04,120 --> 00:27:07,667
And I'm going to try to
talk about the contributions

634
00:27:07,667 --> 00:27:08,500
as much as possible.

635
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:10,208
I might go through
this a little quickly.

636
00:27:10,208 --> 00:27:12,673
So we have time for questions.

637
00:27:12,673 --> 00:27:14,590
The first one is a
controllable dialogue model

638
00:27:14,590 --> 00:27:16,090
that conditions
on the game state

639
00:27:16,090 --> 00:27:18,250
and a set of intended
actions for the speaker

640
00:27:18,250 --> 00:27:19,090
and the recipient.

641
00:27:19,090 --> 00:27:21,750

642
00:27:21,750 --> 00:27:24,223
So I have a question,
what is the action

643
00:27:24,223 --> 00:27:25,265
space here for the model?

644
00:27:25,265 --> 00:27:28,950

645
00:27:28,950 --> 00:27:32,190
The action space for the
action prediction model

646
00:27:32,190 --> 00:27:34,680
is like all the actions that
you could take in the game--

647
00:27:34,680 --> 00:27:37,040
that a player could
take in the game.

648
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:38,790
For the dialogue model,
it's like messages

649
00:27:38,790 --> 00:27:40,960
that you can send.

650
00:27:40,960 --> 00:27:41,680
Got it.

651
00:27:41,680 --> 00:27:43,514
So-- one second.

652
00:27:43,514 --> 00:27:47,420

653
00:27:47,420 --> 00:27:47,920
OK.

654
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:51,580
So we train what we call
an intent model that

655
00:27:51,580 --> 00:27:53,650
predicts what actions
people will take

656
00:27:53,650 --> 00:27:55,030
at the end of truthful turns.

657
00:27:55,030 --> 00:27:58,090
Basically, what we're trying
to predict, what are people

658
00:27:58,090 --> 00:28:03,410
intending to do when they
communicate a certain message?

659
00:28:03,410 --> 00:28:07,070
And then we use this to
automatically annotate

660
00:28:07,070 --> 00:28:09,470
the data set with
basically what we

661
00:28:09,470 --> 00:28:12,800
expect people's intentions were
when they sent that message.

662
00:28:12,800 --> 00:28:15,753
And we filter out--

663
00:28:15,753 --> 00:28:17,920
we filter out as much as
possible lies from the data

664
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:22,180
set, so that the
text of the data set

665
00:28:22,180 --> 00:28:26,170
is annotated with the
truthful intention.

666
00:28:26,170 --> 00:28:29,070

667
00:28:29,070 --> 00:28:31,770
And then during play, Cicero
conditions the dialogue model

668
00:28:31,770 --> 00:28:34,500
on the truthful intention
that it intends to take.

669
00:28:34,500 --> 00:28:36,750
And the goal then is
that-- the hope then

670
00:28:36,750 --> 00:28:39,030
is that it will
generate a message

671
00:28:39,030 --> 00:28:41,280
consistent with that intention.

672
00:28:41,280 --> 00:28:46,620
And that is then fed
into everything else.

673
00:28:46,620 --> 00:28:49,170
Sorry, the intentions that
we generate through planning

674
00:28:49,170 --> 00:28:50,640
are fed into the dialogue model.

675
00:28:50,640 --> 00:28:54,780

676
00:28:54,780 --> 00:28:57,520
So to give you an example
of what this looks like,

677
00:28:57,520 --> 00:28:59,940
this gives us a way to
control the dialogue model

678
00:28:59,940 --> 00:29:02,130
through a set of intentions.

679
00:29:02,130 --> 00:29:07,590
Here, we are-- Cicero
is England in pink

680
00:29:07,590 --> 00:29:10,605
and their action is to move to
Belgium, among other things.

681
00:29:10,605 --> 00:29:12,915

682
00:29:12,915 --> 00:29:15,040
And so, if we feed this
intention into the dialogue

683
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:17,170
model, then the message
that might get generated

684
00:29:17,170 --> 00:29:19,570
is something like
England saying to France,

685
00:29:19,570 --> 00:29:21,047
do you mind supporting me--

686
00:29:21,047 --> 00:29:22,630
do you mind supporting
Edi to Belgium?

687
00:29:22,630 --> 00:29:25,690

688
00:29:25,690 --> 00:29:29,980
On the other hand, let's
say Cicero's action is

689
00:29:29,980 --> 00:29:33,600
to support France into Belgium.

690
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:35,940
Then if you feed that
into the dialogue model,

691
00:29:35,940 --> 00:29:37,528
then the message
that's generated

692
00:29:37,528 --> 00:29:39,570
might say something like,
let me know if you want

693
00:29:39,570 --> 00:29:40,710
me to support you to Belgium.

694
00:29:40,710 --> 00:29:42,293
Otherwise, I'll
probably poke Holland.

695
00:29:42,293 --> 00:29:46,470

696
00:29:46,470 --> 00:29:48,660
Now, what we find is that
conditioning the dialogue

697
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:50,760
model on these
intentions in this way,

698
00:29:50,760 --> 00:29:52,362
it makes the model
more controllable,

699
00:29:52,362 --> 00:29:53,820
but it also leads
to higher quality

700
00:29:53,820 --> 00:29:55,680
dialogue with less nonsense.

701
00:29:55,680 --> 00:29:58,333
So we found that
it led to dialogue

702
00:29:58,333 --> 00:30:00,000
that was more consistent
with the state,

703
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,370
more consistent with the
plan, higher quality, lower

704
00:30:02,370 --> 00:30:03,510
perplexity.

705
00:30:03,510 --> 00:30:05,967
And I think the argument--
the reasoning for why

706
00:30:05,967 --> 00:30:07,800
this is the case is
that we're kind of we're

707
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:11,430
leaving the dialogue model the
burden of having to come up

708
00:30:11,430 --> 00:30:13,860
with a good strategy.

709
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:15,360
We're allowing
the dialogue model

710
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:17,193
to do what it does best,
to focus on what it

711
00:30:17,193 --> 00:30:20,220
does best, which is dialogue.

712
00:30:20,220 --> 00:30:23,280
And we're relieving it of
the strategic components

713
00:30:23,280 --> 00:30:24,072
of the game.

714
00:30:24,072 --> 00:30:26,280
Because we're feeding that
strategy into the dialogue

715
00:30:26,280 --> 00:30:26,780
model.

716
00:30:26,780 --> 00:30:31,038

717
00:30:31,038 --> 00:30:32,580
So that's one main
contribution, this

718
00:30:32,580 --> 00:30:36,720
control the dialogue model
that conditions on a plan.

719
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:38,220
The second is a
planning engine that

720
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:40,185
accounts for dialogue
and human behavior.

721
00:30:40,185 --> 00:30:43,000

722
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:49,270
So I mentioned that a lot
of previous work on games

723
00:30:49,270 --> 00:30:54,640
was done using self-play in
two-player zero-sum settings.

724
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:58,450
Now, the problem
with pure self-play

725
00:30:58,450 --> 00:31:00,250
is that it can learn
strong policies

726
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:03,580
but it doesn't stick
with human conventions,

727
00:31:03,580 --> 00:31:05,170
and it can't account
for dialogue.

728
00:31:05,170 --> 00:31:08,170
It's just going to ignore the
human data and the human way

729
00:31:08,170 --> 00:31:12,110
of playing if you
just do self-play.

730
00:31:12,110 --> 00:31:14,300
So that's one extreme.

731
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:15,700
The other extreme
that you can go

732
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:19,990
is to just do supervised
learning on human data,

733
00:31:19,990 --> 00:31:22,120
create this model
of how humans play,

734
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:28,380
and then train with
those imitation humans.

735
00:31:28,380 --> 00:31:30,080
And if you do
this, you'll end up

736
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:31,830
with a bot that's
consistent with dialogue

737
00:31:31,830 --> 00:31:33,060
and human conventions.

738
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:35,640
But it's only as strong
as the training data.

739
00:31:35,640 --> 00:31:37,320
And we found that
it was actually

740
00:31:37,320 --> 00:31:40,920
very easily manipulable
through adversarial dialogue.

741
00:31:40,920 --> 00:31:43,470
So for example, you can
send messages to it saying,

742
00:31:43,470 --> 00:31:45,480
thanks for agreeing to
support me at the Paris.

743
00:31:45,480 --> 00:31:48,360
And it will think
like, well, I've

744
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:50,463
only ever seen that
message in my training data

745
00:31:50,463 --> 00:31:52,630
when I've agreed to support
the person to the Paris.

746
00:31:52,630 --> 00:31:55,047
And so I guess I'm supporting
them at the Paris this turn,

747
00:31:55,047 --> 00:31:57,450
even though that might be a
terrible move for the bot.

748
00:31:57,450 --> 00:31:59,837

749
00:31:59,837 --> 00:32:01,670
So I came up with this
algorithm called piKL

750
00:32:01,670 --> 00:32:05,055
that it kind of is a happy
medium between these two

751
00:32:05,055 --> 00:32:05,555
extremes.

752
00:32:05,555 --> 00:32:08,290

753
00:32:08,290 --> 00:32:13,570
The way piKL works is it's
basically trying to-- it's

754
00:32:13,570 --> 00:32:17,710
doing self-play but
regularized toward sticking

755
00:32:17,710 --> 00:32:20,110
to the human imitation policy.

756
00:32:20,110 --> 00:32:23,110
So it has a KL
penalty for deviating

757
00:32:23,110 --> 00:32:27,370
from the human imitation policy.

758
00:32:27,370 --> 00:32:29,380
So we have this
parameter lambda that

759
00:32:29,380 --> 00:32:33,550
controls how easy
it is to deviate

760
00:32:33,550 --> 00:32:36,640
from the human
invitation policy.

761
00:32:36,640 --> 00:32:40,810
At lambda equals zero, it just
ignores the human imitation

762
00:32:40,810 --> 00:32:43,960
policy completely and
just does pure self-play.

763
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:47,530
And so it just do self-play
as if from scratch

764
00:32:47,530 --> 00:32:49,090
at lambda equals zero.

765
00:32:49,090 --> 00:32:51,250
At lambda equals
infinity, it's just

766
00:32:51,250 --> 00:32:53,140
playing the human
invitation policy

767
00:32:53,140 --> 00:32:57,235
and not doing self-play at all.

768
00:32:57,235 --> 00:32:59,360
But for intermediate values
of lambda, what we find

769
00:32:59,360 --> 00:33:01,340
is that it actually
gives you a good medium

770
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:03,890
between sticking to
human conventions

771
00:33:03,890 --> 00:33:04,940
and performing strongly.

772
00:33:04,940 --> 00:33:08,350

773
00:33:08,350 --> 00:33:11,080
So you can kind of see
this behavior emerge here.

774
00:33:11,080 --> 00:33:14,370

775
00:33:14,370 --> 00:33:15,320
So there's a question.

776
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:18,180
Is this similar to offline RL or
also incorporates exploration?

777
00:33:18,180 --> 00:33:19,680
So let's say, there's
actually a lot

778
00:33:19,680 --> 00:33:25,170
of similar work on
having a KL penalty.

779
00:33:25,170 --> 00:33:27,360
And so yes, I would
say that it's very

780
00:33:27,360 --> 00:33:30,032
similar to a lot of that work.

781
00:33:30,032 --> 00:33:31,740
It's also been done
actually in AlphaStar

782
00:33:31,740 --> 00:33:33,450
where they had a KL penalty.

783
00:33:33,450 --> 00:33:36,450
Though that was more
about aiding exploration,

784
00:33:36,450 --> 00:33:38,430
like using human data
to aid exploration,

785
00:33:38,430 --> 00:33:41,398
rather than trying to
better imitate humans.

786
00:33:41,398 --> 00:33:43,440
So I think what's interesting
about the piKL work

787
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:45,480
is that, one, we
find it imitates

788
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:48,990
humans better than just doing
supervised learning alone.

789
00:33:48,990 --> 00:33:53,600
And two, we are doing
a bit of theory of mind

790
00:33:53,600 --> 00:33:56,840
where we assume that the
other players are also--

791
00:33:56,840 --> 00:34:00,643
we're using this as a model for
our behavior, what we expect

792
00:34:00,643 --> 00:34:02,060
other people to
think our behavior

793
00:34:02,060 --> 00:34:04,850
is in addition to modeling
the other players.

794
00:34:04,850 --> 00:34:09,429
So it's like a
common knowledge--

795
00:34:09,429 --> 00:34:12,159
common knowledge
like an algorithm

796
00:34:12,159 --> 00:34:13,420
that we're using here.

797
00:34:13,420 --> 00:34:17,830

798
00:34:17,830 --> 00:34:20,050
So the kind of behavior
that you see from this,

799
00:34:20,050 --> 00:34:23,199
you can see here, let's
say England agrees--

800
00:34:23,199 --> 00:34:25,120
sorry, so let's say
we're in this situation.

801
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:27,850
This actually came
up in a real game.

802
00:34:27,850 --> 00:34:30,960
And it inspired a
figure from our paper.

803
00:34:30,960 --> 00:34:34,980
So England and
France are fighting.

804
00:34:34,980 --> 00:34:36,120
France is the bot.

805
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:42,030
And France asks if England
is willing to disengage.

806
00:34:42,030 --> 00:34:45,210
And let's say
England says, yes, I

807
00:34:45,210 --> 00:34:48,440
will move out of English
channel if you head back to NAO.

808
00:34:48,440 --> 00:34:53,750
Well, we can see that Cicero
does in fact back off, leaves--

809
00:34:53,750 --> 00:34:57,090
goes to NAO, and the
disengagement is successful.

810
00:34:57,090 --> 00:34:59,720
And so this shows that
the bot strategy really

811
00:34:59,720 --> 00:35:01,220
is reflecting the
dialogue that it's

812
00:35:01,220 --> 00:35:02,870
had with this other player.

813
00:35:02,870 --> 00:35:05,450

814
00:35:05,450 --> 00:35:07,160
Another message that
England might send

815
00:35:07,160 --> 00:35:08,660
is something like,
I'm sorry, you've

816
00:35:08,660 --> 00:35:10,035
been fighting me
this whole game.

817
00:35:10,035 --> 00:35:12,290
I can't trust you that
you won't stab me.

818
00:35:12,290 --> 00:35:15,960
And so in this case, Cicero will
continue its attack on England.

819
00:35:15,960 --> 00:35:17,960
And you can see again,
this is reflective-- it's

820
00:35:17,960 --> 00:35:19,960
changing its behavior
depending on the dialogue.

821
00:35:19,960 --> 00:35:22,410

822
00:35:22,410 --> 00:35:24,160
But you can also have
this kind of message

823
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,555
where England, says,
yes, I'll leave

824
00:35:26,555 --> 00:35:29,180
English channel if you move into
Munich and Holland to Belgium.

825
00:35:29,180 --> 00:35:32,900
So these are really bad
moves for Cicero to follow.

826
00:35:32,900 --> 00:35:36,760
And so if you just use-- if
you just look at the raw policy

827
00:35:36,760 --> 00:35:40,640
net, it might actually do this.

828
00:35:40,640 --> 00:35:43,960
It might actually do these moves
because England suggested it.

829
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:46,822
But because we're using
piKL that incorporates--

830
00:35:46,822 --> 00:35:49,030
it counts for these expected
value different actions,

831
00:35:49,030 --> 00:35:51,700
it will actually partially
back off but ignore

832
00:35:51,700 --> 00:35:53,455
the suggested moves
because it recognize

833
00:35:53,455 --> 00:35:55,705
that those will leave it
very vulnerable to an attack.

834
00:35:55,705 --> 00:36:00,942

835
00:36:00,942 --> 00:36:02,150
I'll skip the slide for time.

836
00:36:02,150 --> 00:36:08,830

837
00:36:08,830 --> 00:36:11,165
Another thing I should
say is that we're not just

838
00:36:11,165 --> 00:36:11,790
doing planning.

839
00:36:11,790 --> 00:36:14,248
We're actually doing this in
a full self-play reinforcement

840
00:36:14,248 --> 00:36:16,410
learning loop.

841
00:36:16,410 --> 00:36:19,380
And again, the goal here is--

842
00:36:19,380 --> 00:36:21,810
it's really about modeling
humans better than supervised

843
00:36:21,810 --> 00:36:22,230
learning alone.

844
00:36:22,230 --> 00:36:24,397
And we found that doing
this self-play reinforcement

845
00:36:24,397 --> 00:36:27,972
learning with piKL allowed us
to better model human behavior

846
00:36:27,972 --> 00:36:29,430
than just doing
imitation learning.

847
00:36:29,430 --> 00:36:32,050

848
00:36:32,050 --> 00:36:34,550
Finally, we have an ensemble
of message filtering techniques

849
00:36:34,550 --> 00:36:36,860
that filters both nonsensical
and strategically unsound

850
00:36:36,860 --> 00:36:37,360
messages.

851
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:39,840

852
00:36:39,840 --> 00:36:42,720
So to give you an example of
what these filters look like,

853
00:36:42,720 --> 00:36:45,520
one that we developed is
value-based filtering.

854
00:36:45,520 --> 00:36:49,460
So the motivation
for this is that what

855
00:36:49,460 --> 00:36:51,920
we feed into our
dialogue model is

856
00:36:51,920 --> 00:36:55,100
a plan for ourselves and
for our speaking partner,

857
00:36:55,100 --> 00:36:58,290
but it's the entire plan
that we have for ourselves.

858
00:36:58,290 --> 00:37:00,825
And so we might end up feeding
into the dialogue model

859
00:37:00,825 --> 00:37:03,200
the fact that we're going to
attack the player that we're

860
00:37:03,200 --> 00:37:04,460
speaking to.

861
00:37:04,460 --> 00:37:07,970
Now, the dialogue model is,
to be honest, kind of dumb.

862
00:37:07,970 --> 00:37:11,180
And it doesn't really
know that it shouldn't

863
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:14,060
be telling this
player that they're

864
00:37:14,060 --> 00:37:15,622
going to be attacked this turn.

865
00:37:15,622 --> 00:37:17,080
And so you have
these messages that

866
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:20,020
might be sent, something like
the second one shown here

867
00:37:20,020 --> 00:37:22,610
where England says to France,
we have hostile intentions

868
00:37:22,610 --> 00:37:23,110
towards you.

869
00:37:23,110 --> 00:37:24,485
You must be wiped
from the board.

870
00:37:24,485 --> 00:37:26,410
Please provide a croissant.

871
00:37:26,410 --> 00:37:29,320
So this is actually a message
that the bot sends to a player.

872
00:37:29,320 --> 00:37:31,860
Not to a player, this was
preliminary testing and kind

873
00:37:31,860 --> 00:37:33,235
of motivated this
whole approach.

874
00:37:33,235 --> 00:37:36,047

875
00:37:36,047 --> 00:37:38,380
So we don't want the bot to
send these kinds of messages

876
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:39,400
if it's going to
attack a player.

877
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:40,858
We want it to send
something that's

878
00:37:40,858 --> 00:37:44,088
not an outright lie necessarily,
but just something--

879
00:37:44,088 --> 00:37:45,880
either not send a
message or send something

880
00:37:45,880 --> 00:37:49,470
that's much more bland.

881
00:37:49,470 --> 00:37:51,600
And so we filter out
these kinds of messages

882
00:37:51,600 --> 00:37:54,960
by looking at the value.

883
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:57,840
What we do is we generate a
bunch of candidate messages,

884
00:37:57,840 --> 00:38:01,020
and then we see if we
were to send this message,

885
00:38:01,020 --> 00:38:04,110
what is the behavior
that we would expect

886
00:38:04,110 --> 00:38:06,510
the other players to take?

887
00:38:06,510 --> 00:38:08,580
What actions will
we expect them to do

888
00:38:08,580 --> 00:38:10,050
after we send this message?

889
00:38:10,050 --> 00:38:13,770
And what do they expect we will
do after we send this message?

890
00:38:13,770 --> 00:38:17,210
And then we see, what
is the expected value

891
00:38:17,210 --> 00:38:19,910
of the action that
we intend to take

892
00:38:19,910 --> 00:38:23,260
given the prediction of what
everybody else is going to do?

893
00:38:23,260 --> 00:38:27,070
So if our intention
is to attack France,

894
00:38:27,070 --> 00:38:29,770
then we can see, well, if I were
to send this message to France,

895
00:38:29,770 --> 00:38:31,660
then they're going to
get really defensive

896
00:38:31,660 --> 00:38:33,220
and defend against
an attack from us,

897
00:38:33,220 --> 00:38:35,270
and our attack is going
to be unsuccessful.

898
00:38:35,270 --> 00:38:37,660
And so therefore, I probably
shouldn't send this message

899
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:38,170
to them.

900
00:38:38,170 --> 00:38:41,092

901
00:38:41,092 --> 00:38:42,550
And so in this way,
we can actually

902
00:38:42,550 --> 00:38:46,257
filter out messages that
have low expected value.

903
00:38:46,257 --> 00:38:48,090
We found that this
worked surprisingly well.

904
00:38:48,090 --> 00:38:51,100

905
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:54,640
Dialogue examples, I'll
go through one just

906
00:38:54,640 --> 00:38:57,560
for the sake of time.

907
00:38:57,560 --> 00:39:02,050
So here we have Cicero's France.

908
00:39:02,050 --> 00:39:03,640
And Francis saying--

909
00:39:03,640 --> 00:39:07,390
France is conversing with
Turkey, who's a human player.

910
00:39:07,390 --> 00:39:09,850
And they're debating over
who's going to get Tunis,

911
00:39:09,850 --> 00:39:12,307
this territory circled in red.

912
00:39:12,307 --> 00:39:14,640
You can see they both have
fleets next to the territory.

913
00:39:14,640 --> 00:39:17,140
If they both go for it, neither
of them are going to get it.

914
00:39:17,140 --> 00:39:19,150
And so they need to work
out some sort of deal.

915
00:39:19,150 --> 00:39:21,692
So France says, I'll work with
you, but I need Tunis for now.

916
00:39:21,692 --> 00:39:24,540
Turkey says, nope, you've
got to let me have it.

917
00:39:24,540 --> 00:39:26,370
France says, no, I need it.

918
00:39:26,370 --> 00:39:28,800
And then France
suggests, you can

919
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:30,390
take these other
territories instead.

920
00:39:30,390 --> 00:39:33,510
You have Serbia
and Rome to take.

921
00:39:33,510 --> 00:39:35,430
Turkey says, they're
impossible targets.

922
00:39:35,430 --> 00:39:39,642
And then Cicero
suggests specific moves

923
00:39:39,642 --> 00:39:41,850
that would allow Turkey to
capture these territories.

924
00:39:41,850 --> 00:39:46,590
So Cicero says, Greece to
Ionian, Ionian to Iranian.

925
00:39:46,590 --> 00:39:48,600
Turkey says, hmm, you're
right, good ideas.

926
00:39:48,600 --> 00:39:49,920
And then France says,
and then in the fall,

927
00:39:49,920 --> 00:39:51,550
you take Rome and
Austria collapses.

928
00:39:51,550 --> 00:39:55,770
And so that allows Turkey to
make progress against Austria,

929
00:39:55,770 --> 00:40:00,270
but conveniently it also
allows France to capture Tunis

930
00:40:00,270 --> 00:40:02,910
because Turkey will be using
those units for something else.

931
00:40:02,910 --> 00:40:06,480

932
00:40:06,480 --> 00:40:08,300
So limitations and
future directions,

933
00:40:08,300 --> 00:40:11,310
intent representation is
just an action per player.

934
00:40:11,310 --> 00:40:13,750
So there's a question
of, the intentions

935
00:40:13,750 --> 00:40:15,500
that we're feeding
into the dialogue model

936
00:40:15,500 --> 00:40:17,300
is an action that we're
going to take for this turn

937
00:40:17,300 --> 00:40:18,950
and for the next
turn, for ourselves

938
00:40:18,950 --> 00:40:20,550
and for the other player.

939
00:40:20,550 --> 00:40:23,870
But ideally, we would have
a richer set of intentions.

940
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:27,380
We would be able to condition on
things like long-term strategy

941
00:40:27,380 --> 00:40:32,720
or style of communication
or asking questions.

942
00:40:32,720 --> 00:40:34,860
That's one of the
limitations of this approach.

943
00:40:34,860 --> 00:40:38,810
Now of course, the richer you
make the space of intentions,

944
00:40:38,810 --> 00:40:40,730
the more room there is
for things to go wrong.

945
00:40:40,730 --> 00:40:42,830
And you also have to
then train the model

946
00:40:42,830 --> 00:40:47,880
to be able to handle these
wider space of intentions.

947
00:40:47,880 --> 00:40:50,190
There is a question, do you
think the dialogue model is

948
00:40:50,190 --> 00:40:51,780
learning an internal model--

949
00:40:51,780 --> 00:40:55,290
internal world model to be
so good at predicting moves?

950
00:40:55,290 --> 00:41:01,010
No, we're-- this is arguably
why we're conditioning

951
00:41:01,010 --> 00:41:01,940
on intentions.

952
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:03,740
We're relieving
the dialogue model

953
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:06,320
of having to come up
with a good world model

954
00:41:06,320 --> 00:41:08,390
because we're telling
it, these are the moves

955
00:41:08,390 --> 00:41:10,040
that we are planning
to take this turn,

956
00:41:10,040 --> 00:41:11,690
and these are the moves that
we would like this other player

957
00:41:11,690 --> 00:41:12,920
to take this turn.

958
00:41:12,920 --> 00:41:18,420
So we're like-- we're able
to have the world model

959
00:41:18,420 --> 00:41:22,105
separate from the dialogue model
but condition on the output

960
00:41:22,105 --> 00:41:22,980
from the world model.

961
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:26,730

962
00:41:26,730 --> 00:41:29,340
Another limitation is
that Cicero's value model

963
00:41:29,340 --> 00:41:30,850
doesn't condition on dialogue.

964
00:41:30,850 --> 00:41:32,628
And so it has a
limited understanding

965
00:41:32,628 --> 00:41:34,170
of the long-term
effects of dialogue.

966
00:41:34,170 --> 00:41:38,800

967
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:44,200
This greatly limits our ability
to plan what kind of messages

968
00:41:44,200 --> 00:41:47,320
we should be sending.

969
00:41:47,320 --> 00:41:50,920
And this is actually why
we always conditioned

970
00:41:50,920 --> 00:41:54,700
Cicero's dialogue generation
on its truthful intentions.

971
00:41:54,700 --> 00:41:57,760
You could argue that there is
situations in Diplomacy where

972
00:41:57,760 --> 00:42:00,580
you would want to lie
to the other player.

973
00:42:00,580 --> 00:42:05,230
The best players rarely lie,
but they do lie sometimes.

974
00:42:05,230 --> 00:42:11,560
And you have to understand the
trade off between if you lie,

975
00:42:11,560 --> 00:42:13,390
you are going to not--

976
00:42:13,390 --> 00:42:14,890
it's going to be
much harder to work

977
00:42:14,890 --> 00:42:17,390
with this person in the future.

978
00:42:17,390 --> 00:42:20,230
And so you have to make sure
that the value that you're

979
00:42:20,230 --> 00:42:23,350
getting positionally is
worth that loss of trust

980
00:42:23,350 --> 00:42:25,570
and that broken relationship.

981
00:42:25,570 --> 00:42:29,360
Now, because
Cicero's value model

982
00:42:29,360 --> 00:42:32,000
doesn't condition on
dialogue, it can't really

983
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:33,600
understand this trade off.

984
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:37,970
And so for this reason, we
actually always condition it

985
00:42:37,970 --> 00:42:40,760
on its truthful intentions.

986
00:42:40,760 --> 00:42:45,600
No, it is possible to
have Cicero's value

987
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:47,970
model conditioned on dialogue,
but you would need way

988
00:42:47,970 --> 00:42:50,770
more data and it would make
things much more expensive.

989
00:42:50,770 --> 00:42:53,910
And so we weren't able to
do it further for this bot.

990
00:42:53,910 --> 00:42:57,710

991
00:42:57,710 --> 00:42:59,882
And finally, there's
a big question

992
00:42:59,882 --> 00:43:01,340
that I mentioned
earlier, which is,

993
00:43:01,340 --> 00:43:04,130
is there a more general way of
scaling inference time compute

994
00:43:04,130 --> 00:43:05,900
to achieve better performance?

995
00:43:05,900 --> 00:43:08,360
The way that we've
done planning in Cicero

996
00:43:08,360 --> 00:43:10,590
is, I would argue, a
bit domain specific.

997
00:43:10,590 --> 00:43:13,137
I think it's like-- the idea
of piKL is quite general.

998
00:43:13,137 --> 00:43:15,470
But I think that there are
potentially more general ways

999
00:43:15,470 --> 00:43:16,460
of doing planning.

1000
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:22,220

1001
00:43:22,220 --> 00:43:23,680
Somebody is asking,
looking forward

1002
00:43:23,680 --> 00:43:24,972
to the next two to three years.

1003
00:43:24,972 --> 00:43:27,400
What criteria will you use
to select the next game

1004
00:43:27,400 --> 00:43:28,960
to try to conquer?

1005
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:30,920
Honestly, like I said,
we chose Diplomacy

1006
00:43:30,920 --> 00:43:32,670
because we thought it
would be the hardest

